A||||5||GarethWright||Squeeking Front and Rear||||1||
Z||000000||GarethWright||11-29-2000||08:12 PM||||Hi, I am having two squeeking problems at the minute on my '87 GT.  I'm getting a really high pitched squeek from my passengers side Front wheel-area, I dont know Exactly where, as I cant have the car moving while I inspect it   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  But, the squeeking stops Instantly when I hit the brakes, no matter how lightly.  Could this be a bearing going out?<BR>Also my Alternator-Water Pump belt is squeeking on acceleration (sometimes it grabs again when I let off the gas, sometimes it keeps squeeking) I have replaced it once when it was squeeking really badly a few months ago.  I have heard that those belts are really sensitive when it comes to tension.  Is there a belt tensioner I can install?  Or do I just have to get it right when I put a new one on?  Any help would be much appreciated as I dont want anything bad to happen (obviously   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )<P>Thanks in advance,<BR>Gareth<p>[This message has been edited by GarethWright (edited 11-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||11-29-2000||08:25 PM||||likely a brake pad dragging. first thing to do is check all the hardware ON ALL 4 WHEELS.<P>Take all the calipers off and regrease the slide bolts.<P>There are also areas on the caliper and inner pad that rub against the bracket. make sure these areas are free of major rust and coat them with grease as well.<P>If all 4 calipers are good this should kill the dragging. <P>NOTE: The inner pad on the rear is held to the face of the piston by a stainless steel clip. if that clip is missing then the pad will drag and/or rattle no matter what you do. The clip is not built into the pad like the front. I've only been able to get replacement clips as part of the hardware kit for the caliper. (2 slides the rubbers and the clip. 1 kit required for each caliper.)<P>If you let this go you could end up warping the rotors from excess heat.||||reg||1
Z||000002||GarethWright||11-29-2000||08:33 PM||||OK I will have to dig out my Haynes Manual and see if I can take care of the brakes tomorrow afternoon.  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ed||11-30-2000||03:03 AM||||You also have wear indicators on the brake pads.  They squeak when the pads are worn, but they stop squeaking when you apply the brakes because of minor deflection. At least until ALL the pad is gone and you're running the bare metal pad backing against your rotors and damaging them.  Then they'll REALLY squeak! <P>So inspect those brakes for wear and the other stuff.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||GarethWright||12-01-2000||07:10 PM||||Well I looked at the brakes, and after reading all those horror stories about the caliper bolts falling out, decided I had better let a professional take care of it.  I need new pads all around, new rotors in the back, and returned rotors on the front.  apparently I also need a new catalytic convertor, which they are also installing for me.<BR>Thanks for the help and info<BR>Gareth||||reg||1
Z||000005||GarethWright||12-01-2000||07:12 PM||||I still need some help with the belt, any advice/stories would be appreciated  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Thanks again<BR>Gareth||||reg||1
